# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# IMPRINT — what does and does not go into the repository.
#
# The one rule that matters here: EVERYTHING UNDER public/ IS COMMITTED.
#
# The hero frames, the brand boards, the monogram tiles and the brand imagery are
# generated locally by Python, Puppeteer and ffmpeg. None of those run on Vercel — the
# deploy runs `next build` and nothing else. If any of public/ were ignored the build
# would still succeed and the deployed site would have no imagery at all, which is the
# worst kind of failure: silent.
#
# Note the leading slashes on /frames/ and /assets/. Without them those patterns match a
# directory of that name at ANY depth, which would swallow public/frames — 180 files and
# the entire hero sequence. Anchoring them to the root is what keeps that from happening,
# and it cannot be undone by a later negation: git will not re-include a file inside an
# ignored directory.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── dependencies and build output
node_modules
.next
out
next-env.d.ts
tsconfig.tsbuildinfo
*.tsbuildinfo

# ── local working directories, anchored to the repo root so public/ is untouched
/frames/
/assets/
/captures/
/BRANDS/

# ── generator scratch. Nothing writes into the repo today — record.mjs uses the system
#    temp dir — but ad-hoc probe scripts get dropped in scripts/ during debugging.
/scripts/_*
/scripts/tmp/
/scripts/out/

# ── python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
venv/

# ── noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.log
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*

# ── local env. There are none today (see DEPLOY.md), but an accidental .env must never
#    reach the repository.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
